WEBVTT

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.310
<v Ira Glass>For WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》节目，我是主持人艾拉·格拉斯。

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:25.920
<v Arnie>I know how it is to beat somebody. I know how it is to pull the trigger, like drive by [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. I know what it is to pull the trigger. I know what it is to stand in the middle of the street, like a stop sign, throwing out gang signs and expecting a bullet to come to you or somebody to just whack you in the head or something.
<v Arnie>我知道打人是什么感觉。我知道扣动扳机是什么感觉，就像驾车枪击一样。我知道扣动扳机是什么滋味。我知道站在街道中央，像个停车标志一样，比划着帮派手势，等待着子弹射向自己，或者等着有人往你头上猛敲一顿是什么感觉。

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:52.420
<v Ira Glass>From the time he was 13 until the time he was 17, Arnie's life was organized around one thing, the Latin Kings, a huge street gang here in Chicago. Then when he was 17, some kids dragged him to youth group and church services until one day, he felt the presence of the lord and he believed. And Arnie quit his gang. He still organized his life around one thing, but now the one thing was God.
<v Ira Glass>从13岁到17岁，阿尼的生活都围绕着一件事：拉丁国王帮，这是芝加哥当地的一个大型街头帮派。17岁那年，一些孩子把他拉到了青年团和教会礼拜，直到有一天，他感受到了上帝的存在并产生了信仰。于是，阿尼退出了帮派。他的生活依然围绕着一件事，但现在这件事变成了上帝。

00:00:52.420 --> 00:01:18.520
<v Ira Glass>But when you quit a street gang, one of the problems that you face is that your former enemies do not know that you've quit. They see you on the street and they come after you. And you don't have your old gang to protect you anymore. There's no way to let everybody know. You can't just publish a notice in the federal register in the classified ads of your local paper. And so guys kept coming up to Arnie on the street trying to fight him or shoot him.
<v Ira Glass>但是，当你退出一个街头帮派时，你要面对的问题之一就是，你以前的敌人并不知道你已经退出了。他们在街上见到你，就会追杀你。而你已经没有以前的帮派来保护你了。没法让每个人都知道。你总不能在联邦公报或当地报纸的分类广告里登个声明吧。所以，人们还是会不断在街上找阿尼，想要打他或开枪射他。

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:23.940
<v Ira Glass>Once, for example, some gang kids came up and Arnie happened to be walking with a guy who was a friend of theirs.
<v Ira Glass>比如有一次，几个帮派的孩子冲过来，而阿尼碰巧正和一个他们的朋友走在一起。

00:01:23.940 --> 00:02:08.170
<v Arnie>So he jumped in front of me before they were going to shoot me and he goes, no, he's a Christian now. He's a man of God. He's not a King anymore. That's the [UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]. And the guy was like, no way, I know him. He ain't no Christian. He was like move and then he was going to hit me, right? I was ready to start hitting people and just run. You know just elbow him here, punch him here, and make so I can get out. It's like my mind was ready, boom, take control. Just start fighting. But I just like, wait a minute. No, I'm not going to do that. God has changed me. I'm a new creation like it says in the bible. Therefore, anybody who's in Christ a new creation, the old has gone, the new has come. And I am the new that has come.
<v Arnie>他跳到我面前，在他们要开枪之前说：“不，他现在是基督徒了。他是上帝的人。他不再是国王帮的人了。那是……”那个家伙说：“不可能，我认识他。他才不是什么基督徒。”他喊着让他走开，然后就想打我，对吧？我当时已经准备好要揍人了，然后就跑。你知道，肘击这里，拳击这里，好让自己脱身。我的大脑当时准备好了，“砰”的一声，掌控局面。直接开始打架。但我对自己说，“等一下。不，我不能那样做。上帝改变了我。就像圣经里说的，我是个新造的人。因此，任何在基督里的人，都是新造的人，旧事已过，一切都变成新的了。而我就是那个重获新生的人。”

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:34.390
<v Arnie>I just looked around. I was just like, hey. I just said, look, I'm a man of God now. You do what you got to do. You know what I'm saying? But I don't gang bang no more. And soon I hope to see you in our church praise the lord together. So he was like, man. So he got mad, real mad. He just said, forget you, man. And he walked away and all his boys walked back to the car and they didn't do nothing to me.
<v Arnie>我只是环顾四周，对他们说：“嘿，听着，我现在是上帝的人了。你们想做什么就做吧。你们懂我的意思吗？但我不再参与帮派斗争了。我希望很快能在我们的教堂见到你们，一起赞美主。”他听了之后很生气，非常生气。他只是说：“去你的吧，伙计。”然后就走开了，他所有的手下也回到了车里，他们没对我做任何事。

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:53.910
<v Ira Glass>Arnie told me a lot of these stories. And they're all like stories from the Bible. The stories where Paul goes out among the non-believers and they want to hurt him and he's filled with the holy spirit, the bible says. And then for some reason, because I guess he's filled with the holy spirit, they do not hurt him. Sometimes Arnie think his life is lot like those stories.
<v Ira Glass>阿尼跟我讲过很多这样的故事。它们就像圣经里的故事一样。保罗在非信徒中传道，他们想要伤害他，但圣经说他心中充满了圣灵。然后出于某种原因，我想是因为他充满了圣灵，他们就没有伤害他。有时阿尼觉得他的生活就像那些故事一样。

00:02:53.910 --> 00:03:59.650
<v Arnie>The one I like the best is Stephen in the Book of Acts when he got stoned. I love that story because I feel like Stephen is me. Yeah, like I'm Stephen. I'm not, but like we got the same spirits, which we do. How they have him ready to persecute him and he's like, he's not afraid to tell the truth. In other words, he's down for his. He's willing to live and die for what he believes in. Just like when you're willing to live and die for gang bang. Like me, I was a Latin King. You know what I'm saying? You got to live and die for that crown, right? And that's what he was dying for. He was dying for God. You know, our lord. He's there, he's been done. He's standing up firm. He's like, I don't care about anybody. Just like when you're on the street. I don't care. I'm a Latin King. What's up? You know, I don't care. What's up? They beat their chests with their gang sign. You know, what's up? What's up? I'm this and this and that. They're not afraid or ashamed to say who they are.
<v Arnie>我最喜欢的是《使徒行传》中司提反被乱石打死的故事。我喜欢那个故事，因为我觉得司提反就是我。没错，就像我就是司提反。我虽然不是，但我们有着同样的灵魂，确实如此。他们想要迫害他时，他并不害怕说出真相。换句话说，他为了自己的信念坚守到底。他愿意为自己的信仰而生，为自己的信仰而死。就像当你愿意为帮派火拼而生、而死一样。像我，我以前是拉丁国王帮的。你懂我意思吗？你必须为那个王冠而活、为它而死，对吧？而他就是为了上帝而死。你知道，我们的主。他在那儿，他已经完成了。他坚定地站着。他就像是说：“我不在乎任何人。就像你在街上一样。我不在乎。我是拉丁国王帮的。怎么着？你知道，我不在乎。怎么着？”他们拍着胸脯，比划着帮派手势。你知道，“怎么着？怎么着？我是谁谁谁。”他们不害怕，也不为自己的身份感到羞耻。

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:26.530
<v Ira Glass>There are people for whom everything in the world comes down to one thing. One idea, one belief, and they organize their whole understanding of the world around that belief. The belief might be God, or free market economics, or animal rights, or the ozone layer. And some people structure their lives around collecting old records, or around sex, or theater, or politics, or Star Trek , or basketball.
<v Ira Glass>这世上有些人，万事万物对他们来说都归结为一件事。一个理念，一个信仰，他们将自己对世界的全部理解都组织在这个信仰周围。这个信仰可能是上帝，可能是自由市场经济，可能是动物权利，也可能是臭氧层。有些人则将生活围绕着收集旧唱片，或者是性、戏剧、政治、星际迷航或篮球而构建。

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:39.990
<v Ira Glass>I've been a one thing person. Maybe you've been a one thing person too. Arnie certainly was. And his new one thing, the lord, filled the same place in his life that the old one thing, the gang, used to.
<v Ira Glass>我曾经也是个“一件事”主义者。也许你也是。阿尼肯定是的。而他现在的新“一件事”——上帝，在他生命中占据了和旧的“一件事”——帮派，曾经占据的一模一样的位置。

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:51.930
<v Arnie>You know, sometimes I want to give it all, just like I give it all to my gang. I want to give it all to him. Even double, you know. I want to die for the lord. I don't care if I get crucified. I don't care if they shoot me.
<v Arnie>你知道，有时我想付出一切，就像我曾经为帮派付出一切那样。我想把一切都献给他。甚至是双倍的，你知道。我想为上帝而死。我不在乎是否被钉死在十字架上。我不在乎他们是否向我开枪。

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.580
<v Ira Glass>You feel the same way about Christianity as you did about the gang?
<v Ira Glass>你对基督教的感受和你对帮派的感受一样吗？

00:04:54.580 --> 00:05:03.660
<v Arnie>Yeah, exactly. It's like I have the same mentality and the same heart, but now it's for a different reason and a different person.
<v Arnie>是的，完全一样。就像我有着同样的心态和同样的内心，但现在是为了不同的理由，为了不同的人。

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:12.730
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, One Thing People. People who organize their lives around just one idea and the price they pay for that.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们要聊聊“一件事”主义者。那些将生活完全围绕一个理念组织起来的人，以及他们为此付出的代价。

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:16.370
<v Ira Glass>Act One, when your one thing is your ex-girlfriend.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，当你的“一件事”是你的前女友时。

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:29.570
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Inescapable Logic. What happens when you take a utopian vision of nudism, yes, nudism, to its logical end as your one thing. This is the true story of a nude presidential candidate.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，不可逃避的逻辑。当你把裸体主义——没错，就是裸体主义——的乌托邦愿景作为你唯一的追求，并将其推向逻辑尽头时，会发生什么？这是一个裸体总统候选人的真实故事。

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:40.570
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, more than one thing. How journalist Philip Weiss came to agree with some Clinton conspiracy theorists, but decided not to follow their cause.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，不止一件事。记者菲利普·韦斯如何开始认同克林顿阴谋论者的一些观点，却决定不去追随他们的事业。

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:52.120
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Quitting Quitting. Regrets from a former one-thinger. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，戒掉戒除。一个曾经的“一件事”主义者的悔悟。请继续收听。

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:55.780
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Life Without Leanne.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，没有莉安的生活。

00:05:55.780 --> 00:06:08.640
<v Ira Glass>Well, let's start our show with the kind of one thing behavior most people at some point or another have gone through themselves. Taken, perhaps just a step or two further than most of us have taken it.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，让我们从大多数人在某种程度上都经历过的那种“一件事”行为开始我们今天的节目。只不过，这比我们大多数人走得稍微远了那么一点。

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:27.490
<v Larry Doyle>Good evening and welcome to Life Without Leanne. I'm Larry and this is day 683. That's nearly 98 weeks since Leanne and I broke up.
<v Larry Doyle>晚上好，欢迎收听《没有莉安的生活》。我是拉里，今天是第683天。距离我和莉安分手已经快98周了。

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:39.340
<v Larry Doyle>We're going to start out, as always, with our Leanne watcher of the week. My kudos go out to Mike of Evanston, Illinois, who called in with this eloquent report on a brief encounter he had with Leanne on April 28.
<v Larry Doyle>我们要像往常一样，先请出我们本周的“莉安观察员”。我要向伊利诺伊州埃文斯顿的迈克致敬，他打来电话，讲述了4月28日他与莉安的一次短暂邂逅，报告非常精彩。

00:06:39.340 --> 00:07:15.220
<v Mike>Hey, Larry. I love the show. Listen, I ran into Leanne last week. She lost some weight, but she's still beautiful. She said she's been exercising, taking classes, doing this, doing that. But it appeared to me that she was struggling to fill some void. Your name didn't come up, but it wasn't so much what she said as what she didn't say.
<v Mike>嘿，拉里。我喜欢这个节目。听着，我上周遇到了莉安。她瘦了一些，但依然很漂亮。她说她一直在锻炼，参加各种课程，做这做那。但在我看来，她是在努力填补某种空虚。她没提到你的名字，但重点不在于她说了什么，而在于她没说什么。

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:25.240
<v Larry Doyle>Thanks, Mike. I think we all know what she was trying to say.
<v Larry Doyle>谢谢你，迈克。我想我们都知道她想表达什么。

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:51.340
<v Larry Doyle>I wish it all could be such good news. But unfortunately, Operation Terrible Mistake has not been the success that we anticipated and I'm afraid we may have to rethink our strategy. As you may recall from our April 20 program, the objectives of Operation Terrible Mistake were to one, apply societal pressure. Two, foster emotional uncertainty. Three, precipitate reevaluation. And ideally, four, achieve reconciliation.
<v Larry Doyle>我希望一切都能有这么好的消息。但不幸的是，“恐怖错误行动”并没有像我们预期的那样成功，恐怕我们得重新考虑策略了。正如你们可能还记得我们在4月20日节目中提到的，“恐怖错误行动”的目标是：第一，施加社会压力；第二，制造情绪上的不确定性；第三，促使她重新评估；理想情况下，第四，实现复合。

00:07:51.340 --> 00:08:15.400
<v Larry Doyle>When I brought up this plan, I suggested the following conversation starter when you would run into Leanne on the street or something. I suggested that you say something like, "Leanne, I was so sorry to hear about you and Larry. You make such a wonderful couple, so I don't mind telling you I think you are making a terrible mistake. This is my own personal opinion on the matter."
<v Larry Doyle>当我提出这个计划时，我建议在街上或其他地方偶遇莉安时使用以下对话开头。我建议你这样说：“莉安，听说你和拉里的事我很遗憾。你们曾是一对多么棒的伴侣，所以我不介意告诉你，我认为你犯了一个严重的错误。这是我个人的看法。”

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:54.410
<v Larry Doyle>Now unfortunately, a number of well meaning individuals took this suggestion rather more literally than I intended and repeated it verbatim to Leanne. And some of it sounds like you were practicing it and it created an effect other than the one I desired. I've now received word through an intermediary that Leanne request that I quote, "Call off the zombies," unquote. We will honor her wishes as always. But I must emphasize, I cannot be held responsible for the behavior of individuals acting on their own initiative.
<v Larry Doyle>不幸的是，一些善意的人把这个建议执行得比我预想的更字面化，并一字不差地复述给了莉安。而且有些人听起来像是在演习，这造成了我所期望之外的效果。我现在通过中间人收到消息，莉安要求我，引用她的话，“叫停这些僵尸”。我们将一如既往地尊重她的意愿。但我必须强调，对于那些自作主张的个人行为，我不承担任何责任。

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:58.470
<v Larry Doyle>When Leanne was Little, number 36 in the series.
<v Larry Doyle>《莉安小时候》，系列第36集。

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:24.790
<v Larry Doyle>At our regular Monday lunch, Leanne's mother told me a cute story. She says that when Leanne was seven, she fell while roller skating and badly skinned her knee. There was no permanent scarring fortunately. In an effort to console her, Leanne's mother recalls telling Leanne, if you don't stop crying, your boo-boo is going to think you don't like him. And that's why, to this day, Leanne endearingly refers to minor cuts and scrapes as Mr. Boo.
<v Larry Doyle>在我们固定的周一午餐会上，莉安的母亲给我讲了一个可爱的故事。她说莉安七岁时，滑旱冰摔倒，膝盖擦伤得很严重。幸运的是没有留下永久疤痕。为了安慰她，莉安的妈妈记得自己告诉莉安：“如果你再不停止哭泣，你的伤口会觉得你不喜欢它。”这就是为什么直到今天，莉安还会亲昵地把小伤口和小擦伤称为“布先生”。

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:45.680
<v Larry Doyle>I've got the minutes to the most recent Leanne Anonymous Meeting, which I'll read to you now. It was our first meeting at Gatsby's. The bartender, Mark, graciously accommodated us by closing off the back room and supplying extra folding chairs. All in attendance praised the wisdom of moving these meetings from my apartment, which some of them had complained was not in neutral territory and it was getting kind of cramped in any case.
<v Larry Doyle>我拿到了最近一次“莉安匿名互助会”的会议记录，现在读给你们听。这是我们在盖茨比酒吧举行的第一次会议。酒保马克非常慷慨，通过关掉后厅并提供额外的折叠椅来安置我们。所有参加者都称赞将会议从我的公寓搬出来的明智之举，之前有人抱怨那里不是中立地带，而且无论如何，那地方也确实太拥挤了。

00:09:45.680 --> 00:10:05.580
<v Larry Doyle>On a related matter, Mark told me privately after the meeting that he appreciates our patronage, but he asks that in the future we try not to monopolize the jukebox. Or at least try to play a variety of songs. He's threatening to remove all the Hank Williams selections if he doesn't see some improvement. So let's all try to work some Patsy Cline in there or something, OK?
<v Larry Doyle>相关的事，马克在会后私下告诉我，他很感激我们的光顾，但要求我们在未来尽量不要霸占点唱机。或者至少尝试播放多种多样的歌曲。他威胁说，如果看不到改进，就要撤掉所有的汉克·威廉姆斯精选。所以，大家都试着在里面加入一些帕齐·克莱恩的歌，好吗？

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:20.540
<v Larry Doyle>Meeting started out. We ordered a first round and at Tom's suggestion, we dispensed with the reading of the minutes. We proceeded immediately to old business. The continuing debate on Leanne's eyes and whether they are turbulent sea green or sand fleck moon blue. They are, by the way, a sand fleck moon blue.
<v Larry Doyle>会议开始了。我们点了第一轮酒，在汤姆的建议下，我们省去了阅读会议记录的环节。直接进入旧议题。关于莉安眼睛颜色的持续争论：到底是波涛汹涌的海绿色，还是沙点月光蓝。顺便说一下，它们的颜色是沙点月光蓝。

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:32.310
<v Larry Doyle>But when it appeared there could be no middle ground on the issue, Dick stood up and announced, hey, you know, like Elton John says, who cares if they're blue or they're green. Those are the sweetest eyes I've ever seen.
<v Larry Doyle>但当这个问题似乎无法达成共识时，迪克站起来宣布：“嘿，你们知道的，就像埃尔顿·约翰唱的那样，‘谁在乎它们是蓝是绿，那是我见过最甜美的双眼’。”

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:37.450
<v Larry Doyle>The motion to adopt Dick's language carried unanimously and we collected more change for the jukebox.
<v Larry Doyle>采纳迪克措辞的动议获得一致通过，我们又为点唱机凑了一些零钱。

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:53.120
<v Larry Doyle>We ordered another round and conversation turned naturally to the rest of Leanne. Her quirky, perky nose, her funny, sunny smile, the perfect curve or her neck. Her soft shoulders and so on. Until petty jealousies precluded further discussion.
<v Larry Doyle>我们又点了下一轮酒，谈话自然地转向了莉安的其他部分。她古灵精怪、充满活力的鼻子，她滑稽、阳光的笑容，她颈部完美的曲线。她柔软的肩膀等等。直到小小的嫉妒心阻止了进一步的讨论。

00:10:53.120 --> 00:11:12.030
<v Arnie>Soon thereafter, we took a break to order some more refreshments, and then it was time to welcome new members. This stubby and not particularly attractive man who had been spotted with Leanne as recently as mid January stood up and he said, My name's Harry and I love Leanne.
<v Arnie>此后不久，我们休息了一下，点了些点心，然后是欢迎新成员的时间。这个身材敦实、长相并不出众的男人，曾于1月中旬被发现与莉安在一起，他站起来说：“我叫哈里，我爱莉安。”

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:27.710
<v Arnie>Harry then related his long, sad tale. The details of which we are all too familiar with. He ended with that same old refrain, she met this guy. She says she's deliriously happy. That prompted Gunther, who really doesn't speak very often to speak up.
<v Arnie>哈里随后讲述了他漫长而悲伤的故事，其中的细节我们都已经太熟悉了。他以那句老调重弹结束：“她遇到了那个家伙。她说她现在快乐得要命。”这促使平时不怎么说话的冈瑟插了一句嘴。

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:36.770
<v Arnie>She's deliriously happy, eh?, he said, staring into his beer. That guy is doomed. Those who could still laugh about it, did.
