00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:35.810
<v Larry Doyle>And before they could reach him, a long freight train raced up the track, just as the cops were moving towards Eddie. He timed it perfectly. Waving and nodding he jumped over the tracks right in front of the engine, leaving the cop on the other side. And by the time the freight train had passed, Eddie was gone. He was already running and he was running nude.
<v Larry Doyle>在他们能抓到他之前，一列长长的货运列车就在警察走向埃迪时冲上了轨道。他掐准了时间。挥手点头，他跳过轨道，恰好在火车头前面，把警察留在了另一边。当货运列车经过时，埃迪已经不见了。他已经在奔跑了，而且是赤身裸体地跑着。

00:34:35.810 --> 00:35:13.200
<v Larry Doyle>Eddie ran all the way to Kansas City, mostly at night. And Playboy magazine picked up his story and sent a reporter and a photographer to follow him. The pictures were all of Eddie naked eating banana splits with a bunch of old farm boys at a Stuckey's or posed on the seat of a big, hog Harley at a rest stop surrounded by big, hairy biker guys. Naked except for a McDonald's cap giving out free french fries and nudity now buttons under the shadow of the golden arches. Playboy magazine bailed him out each time he was arrested.
<v Larry Doyle>埃迪一路跑到了堪萨斯城，大部分是在晚上。《花花公子》杂志接手了他的故事，派出一名记者和一名摄影师跟随他。照片里全是埃迪赤身裸体地在斯塔基餐厅和一群老农家男孩吃香蕉船，或者坐在服务区的巨大哈雷摩托车上，周围是高大的、毛茸茸的摩托车手。他除了戴着麦当劳的帽子，在金拱门阴影下分发免费薯条和“现在就裸体”的徽章外，一丝不挂。《花花公子》杂志在他每次被捕时都为他保释。

00:35:13.200 --> 00:35:40.000
<v Larry Doyle>But when he got to Kansas City, Eddie got cagey. This was the big time and he covered himself, his nude body, his kilt, and his t-shirt in a black three-piece suit he bought at the Salvation Army. He borrowed the Playboy writer's press ID and Eddie boldly moved behind Republican lines, making it to the front steps of the convention center. That was close enough.
<v Larry Doyle>但当他到达堪萨斯城时，埃迪变得精明起来。这是关键时刻，他用在救世军买的一套黑色三件套西装遮住了自己——他的裸体、短裙和T恤。他借用了《花花公子》记者的媒体证，埃迪大胆地潜入共和党防线，到达了会展中心的台阶前。那已经足够近了。

00:35:40.000 --> 00:36:17.950
<v Larry Doyle>And the next day, Eddie found a fruit crate and he wrote soap all over it in black magic marker. And he returned to the convention steps, standing up high on the soap box above the crowd. His whole being overwhelmed with the moment, the power, of his big idea. Blurting out verbiage and joy, his clothes flying and hysterical in all directions. And there were brief looks of childlike wonder across Republican faces in the crowd, but then they were replaced with this all to adult frown of disgust and censor.
<v Larry Doyle>第二天，埃迪找了一个水果箱，用黑色记号笔在上面写满“肥皂”。他回到会展中心的台阶上，高高地站在人群上方的肥皂箱上。他的整个人被那一刻、被他宏大想法的力量所淹没。滔滔不绝、欢呼雀跃，他的衣服在各个方向飞舞。人群中共和党人的脸上闪过一丝孩子般的好奇，但很快就被那种成年人特有的厌恶和谴责所取代。

00:36:17.950 --> 00:36:36.640
<v Larry Doyle>The Kansas City cops, hard men who had been put on kook alert, knocked him from the soap box and they slammed him into the cement steps. And they dragged him bodily away. Eddie's anxious cry of nudity now drowned out by police radios and the charging march of police boots.
<v Larry Doyle>堪萨斯城的警察，那些被派去处理疯子的硬汉，把他从肥皂箱上撞了下来，把他摔在水泥台阶上。他们把他拖走了。埃迪焦急的“现在就裸体”的呼喊声被警用无线电和警察靴子整齐的行进声淹没了。

00:36:36.640 --> 00:36:55.390
<v Larry Doyle>And Eddie stayed in jail for the rest of the convention. His name would never appear on the Republican list of nominees that year. And like so many presidential candidates-- Jesse Jackson, Gary Hart, Paul Tsongas, Ross Perot-- the early days of his campaign would prove to be the most memorable.
<v Larry Doyle>埃迪在大会余下的时间里一直待在监狱里。他的名字那一年永远不会出现在共和党的候选人名单上。而且像许多总统候选人一样——杰西·杰克逊、加里·哈特、保罗·宗加斯、罗斯·佩罗——他竞选初期的日子将证明是最令人难忘的。

00:36:55.390 --> 00:37:31.510
<v Larry Doyle>Edward Hickock Collins had peaked too soon. He returned home to The Palace, but he was never quite the same. Eddie's confidence dropped after Kansas City. He had other big moments. Like attending his Yale class reunion football game and dropping his pants and dashing along the sidelines when Yale scored against Harvard. But each one of these episodes led to jail and more hospitals. Or a stream of Nembutals and shock treatments left Eddie struggling to keep a hold on his mind.
<v Larry Doyle>爱德华·希科克·柯林斯达到顶峰太早了。他回到了“宫殿”，但他再也不一样了。埃迪在堪萨斯城之后，信心大减。他还有过其他辉煌时刻。比如参加耶鲁大学的同学会橄榄球赛，脱掉裤子，在耶鲁对阵哈佛时沿着边线奔跑。但这些事件中的每一件都导致他入狱和更多的医院检查。或者，戊巴比妥和休克疗法的洪流让埃迪苦苦挣扎，试图保持清醒。

00:37:31.510 --> 00:37:51.130
<v Larry Doyle>It's strange when you think about it. That nudity would exact this kind of retribution from our society. Nudity. But Eddie stuck to his vision of the world, his one big idea. That a naked man is a free man and should be allowed to be free.
<v Larry Doyle>当你仔细想想，这很奇怪。裸体竟然会从我们的社会中招致这种报复。裸体。但埃迪坚持他看待世界的愿景，他那一个宏大的想法。即一个赤裸的人是一个自由的人，应该被允许自由。

00:37:51.130 --> 00:38:15.750
<v Larry Doyle>The last I heard of him, he had moved back home to Baldwin, Long Island. Eddie began liberating the neighborhood cats and dogs of their leashes, their choke chains, and their flea collars. That's the thing about a messianic vision, about one big idea. If you follow it to its logical conclusion, you don't know where you'll end up.
<v Larry Doyle>我最后一次听到他的消息时，他已经搬回了长岛鲍德温的老家。埃迪开始解放邻里的猫狗，把它们的牵引绳、颈圈和除蚤圈都摘掉。这就是关于救世主愿景、关于一个宏大想法的问题所在。如果你追随它直至逻辑结论，你根本不知道你会走到哪里。

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:39.930
<v Larry Doyle>Eddie ended up reading to his cats and dogs, good old page 201 Cather in The Rye. The dogs and cats listened. They were animals. They had no problem with the F word. Nudity came naturally to them.
<v Larry Doyle>埃迪最终每天给他的猫狗读那本《麦田里的守望者》第201页。猫狗们听着。它们是动物。它们对那个词没有意见。裸体对它们来说是很自然的。

00:38:39.930 --> 00:39:25.180
<v Ira Glass>Chicago playwright Beau O'Reilly.
<v Ira Glass>芝加哥剧作家博·奥赖利。

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:28.750
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, More Than One Thing.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，不止一件事。

00:39:28.750 --> 00:39:40.310
<v Ira Glass>Well, up until now on our program, we've been hearing about eager, full-hearted one-thingers. In this part of the show we turn to reluctant one-thingers. Our first witness, journalist Philip Weiss.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，到目前为止在我们的节目中，我们听到的都是那些充满渴望、全心投入的“一件事”主义者。在节目的这一部分，我们转向那些“不情愿的”主义者。我们的第一位证人，记者菲利普·韦斯。

00:39:40.310 --> 00:40:14.340
<v Ira Glass>Philip Weiss writes for the New York Observer and other publications, and he has spent a lot of time with the Clinton conspiracy theorists. These are the people who see a sinister force, who see one thing behind these events. The death of deputy White House counsel Vince Foster in 1993, the raiding of Foster's office after his death, Travelgate, Web Hubbell and whether he was paid off to keep him from cooperating with prosecutors. Some also include on the list drug and gun running through the Mena airport in Arkansas during Bill Clinton's years as governor, various murders and deaths too numerous to list here.
<v Ira Glass>菲利普·韦斯为《纽约观察家》和其他刊物撰稿，他花了很多时间和克林顿阴谋论者在一起。这些人看到了一种邪恶的力量，他们看到这些事件背后有一件事。1993年白宫副法律顾问文斯·福斯特之死，他死后办公室被搜查，旅游门事件，韦伯·哈贝尔以及他是否被收买以防止其与检察官合作。有些人还将阿肯色州梅纳机场在比尔·克林顿担任州长期间的毒品和枪支走私列入清单，还有不胜枚举的各种谋杀和死亡事件。

00:40:14.340 --> 00:40:31.760
<v Ira Glass>Well, as he did his reporting, Philip Weiss became persuaded by some of the one-thingers. He saw their logic, but he did not become a one-thinger himself. He spoke with This American Life senior producer Paul Tough. Philip Weiss says that before he first went out to meet the Clinton one-thingers, his year with him was fairly unambiguous.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，在他进行报道的过程中，菲利普·韦斯被一些“一件事”主义者说服了。他看到了他们的逻辑，但他自己并没有成为一个“一件事”主义者。他与《美国生活》资深制作人保罗·塔夫进行了交谈。菲利普·韦斯说，在他第一次出去见克林顿的“一件事”主义者之前，他对他们的看法是相当明确的。

00:40:31.760 --> 00:40:44.880
<v Philip Weiss>I thought they were really out to lunch. I thought they were nuts and hung up and obsessed. And had nothing really intelligent to say.
<v Philip Weiss>我觉得他们简直是疯了。我觉得他们很疯狂、执着且沉迷。而且没有一句聪明话。

00:40:44.880 --> 00:40:57.830
<v Paul Tough>So tell me a bit about when you first entered their world in the early weeks or months that you really started dealing with these people. What are they like? What are they like as a group?
<v Paul Tough>那么，告诉我一点当你刚进入他们的世界，在真正开始与这些人打交道的最初几周或几个月里是什么情况。他们是什么样的？作为一个群体，他们怎么样？

00:40:57.830 --> 00:41:39.660
<v Philip Weiss>Well, some of these people meet the Hollywood casting version of an unhappy obsessive. Some of them do. They're very unpleasant. They have weird idiosyncrasies. They're insistent. They're shrill. And you just think, this is one unhappy camper and I want to get out of his personal space as quickly as possible. Having said that, I've met a number of people who are involved in the Clinton scandals who are very happy people, who are engaging their talents fully.
<v Philip Weiss>嗯，这些人里有些人完全符合好莱坞选角版中那种不快乐的强迫症患者。有些人确实如此。他们非常不讨人喜欢。他们有奇怪的特质。他们坚持己见。他们尖刻。你只会觉得，这是一个不快乐的人，我想尽快远离他的个人空间。话说回来，我也遇到过一些参与了克林顿丑闻的人，他们是非常快乐的人，在充分发挥自己的才华。

00:41:39.660 --> 00:42:05.960
<v Paul Tough>For most of the conspiracy theorists, the main event of the Clinton presidency is the death of Vince Foster. The official version of the facts of Foster's death concludes that on July 20, 1993, Vince Foster drove himself to Fort Marcy Park in suburban Virginia, parked his car, walked to a secluded spot, and shot himself. But as Philip Weiss found out, there's another version of the facts, meticulously cataloged in another document, an unofficial one.
<v Paul Tough>对于大多数阴谋论者来说，克林顿总统任期内的主要事件就是文斯·福斯特的死亡。关于福斯特死亡事实的官方说法是，1993年7月20日，文斯·福斯特独自驾车前往弗吉尼亚郊区的马西堡公园，停好车，步行至一个僻静处，然后自杀。但正如菲利普·韦斯发现的那样，还有另一个版本的真相，被细致地归档在另一份文件中，一份非官方的文件。

00:42:05.960 --> 00:43:01.570
<v Philip Weiss>The most important event for me was reading the Sprunt report, which is a classic of sort of call it conspiratorial literature. But it's a study by Hugh Sprunt of Texas, Farmers Branch, Texas, outside Dallas, of the record in the Foster case. And it's brilliant. And when I read that, I realized that the questions that these people have raised about the events in Fort Marcy Park are not trivial and not nuts. They are serious, good, investigative questions.
<v Philip Weiss>对我来说最重要的事件是阅读《斯普朗特报告》，这堪称阴谋论文献中的经典。但它是德克萨斯州法默斯布兰奇的休·斯普朗特对福斯特案记录的研究。它非常精彩。当我读到它时，我意识到这些人对马西堡公园事件提出的问题并非微不足道，也不是胡说八道。那是严肃的、好的调查性问题。

00:43:01.570 --> 00:43:12.790
<v Paul Tough>The most convincing evidence that Philip Weiss found in the Sprunt report had to do with the possibility that Vince Foster died somewhere other than Fort Marcy Park and that his body was moved there by someone.
<v Paul Tough>菲利普·韦斯在《斯普朗特报告》中发现的最具说服力的证据，与文斯·福斯特可能死于马西堡公园以外的地方，且他的尸体被某人转移到那里的可能性有关。

00:43:12.790 --> 00:43:31.770
<v Paul Tough>The report points out that there was no dirt on Foster's shoes, despite the fact that he was supposed to have walked through the park. There were no car keys found in his pocket despite the fact that he was supposed to have driven himself there. There was a missing briefcase, a misquoted witness, eyeglasses too far from the body, and contradictory accounts of what kind of gun was found in Foster's hand.
<v Paul Tough>报告指出，福斯特的鞋子上没有泥土，尽管他本应穿过公园。他的口袋里没有发现车钥匙，尽管他本应是自己开车去的。缺失的公文包、引用错误的证人、离尸体太远的眼镜，以及关于在福斯特手中发现何种枪支的矛盾说法。

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:58.400
<v Philip Weiss>And at this point, I don't have any conclusions beyond the strong feeling that there were shenanigans in Fort Marcy Park that his body was brought there, I feel. He may have shot himself somewhere else. I think he probably did shoot himself. The evidence is, to my mind, pretty strong. But there seemed to have been shenanigans.
<v Philip Weiss>此时此刻，我没有任何结论，除了强烈的感觉——马西堡公园里有猫腻，他的尸体是被带到那里的，我感觉是这样。他可能在其他地方开枪自杀了。我认为他很可能确实是自杀的。证据，在我看来，相当充分。但似乎有猫腻。

00:43:58.400 --> 00:44:31.890
<v Philip Weiss>I went to Arkansas a few times. And many reporters have gone to Arkansas and come back shocked. I came back shocked. I felt like the top of my head was blown off. I just felt that things were different down there and that they explained who our president is politically in a way that was not apparent to people on the East Coast who saw the Rhodes scholar and the Yale law school graduate.
<v Philip Weiss>我去了几次阿肯色州。许多记者去过阿肯色州后回来都感到震惊。我回来时也感到震惊。我觉得头盖骨都被掀开了。我只是觉得那里的事情很不一样，它们以一种东海岸人无法察觉的方式解释了我们的总统在政治上是谁，而东海岸的人们只看到了罗德学者和耶鲁法学院毕业生。

00:44:31.890 --> 00:45:28.470
<v Philip Weiss>I thought for a little while that that would be-- as a journalist and writer, that would be my story. And I was in it for a little while and now I'm not in it. And I don't fully understand why I'm not in it and I was a few months ago. I guess that I believe-- again, while I buy their story about this administration more than I buy the mainstream story, there's a level of alienation in some of the stuff I've read just cruising the 'net that I just wasn't ready for. I just feared what I might become.
<v Philip Weiss>我有一小段时间认为那会是——作为一名记者和作家，那会是我的故事。我在里面待了一小段时间，现在我不在里面了。我不完全明白为什么我不在里面了，明明几个月前我还陷在里面。我想我是相信——虽然我比相信主流说法更相信他们关于这个政府的故事，但我读到的一些东西，在网上闲逛时读到的东西，那种疏离感是我还没准备好的。我只是害怕我会变成什么样。

00:45:28.470 --> 00:45:40.580
<v Philip Weiss>When one announces in respectable company that one no longer accepts the official version of events with respect to Vince Foster's death, people look at you funny.
<v Philip Weiss>当你宣布在体面的公司里，自己不再接受关于文斯·福斯特死亡事件的官方说法时，人们会用奇怪的眼神看你。

00:45:40.580 --> 00:46:01.280
<v Elmo>I remember one conversation with an editor and finally this editor just said, look, Vince Foster committed suicide in Fort Marcy Park, OK? And you know, what can you say?
<v Elmo>我还记得和一位编辑的一次谈话，最后这位编辑只是说：“听着，文斯·福斯特在马西堡公园自杀了，好吗？”你知道，你能说什么呢？

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:26.490
<v Elmo>When your wife is saying to you you're a nut or you've lost perspective. You know, maybe I'm making the wrong call, but that scares me. That threw me.
<v Elmo>当你的妻子对你说你是个疯子，或者你失去了视角时。你知道，也许我做出了错误的判断，但这吓到我了。这让我感到困惑。

00:46:26.490 --> 00:47:00.010
<v Elmo>It's just a question of how much you want to invest yourself in this position, I think. And you pay a price. Your skin is flayed. And that's the traditional price of a heretic. I'm not trying to be self-dramatizing. It's the ancient bargain that society makes with these people. And I don't know if I want to make that investment.
<v Elmo>我觉得这只是一个你想在这个立场上投入多少的问题。而且你要付出代价。你的皮肤被剥掉了。这就是异端的传统代价。我不是试图戏剧化。这是社会与这些人之间古老的契约。我不知道我是否想做出那种投资。

00:47:00.010 --> 00:47:49.760
<v Elmo>One of my first meetings with a person in this community, a guy I've actually become a little friendly with and whom I have a great deal of respect for. And I'm going to call him Patrick because he uses an alias. This is a guy with a very Jimmy Stewart manner and a very casual manner, and he walked me through Fort Marcy Park with great patience, telling me his understanding of what happened on July 20, 1993 when Vince Foster's body was found. And Patrick's story is a very logical one. He had hundreds and thousands of facts at his fingertips that I was and am in no position really to challenge. He's done more research on this than I have.
<v Elmo>我与这个社区中的一个人进行的第一次会面之一，一个我实际上变得有点友好，并且对他有很大尊重的人。我要叫他帕特里克，因为他用了化名。这是一个非常有吉米·斯图尔特风度、态度非常随和的家伙，他耐心地带我走过了马西堡公园，告诉了我他对于1993年7月20日文斯·福斯特尸体被发现时发生的事情的理解。帕特里克的叙述非常合逻辑。他手头有成千上万的事实，我当时和现在都无法真正质疑。他对此所做的研究比我多。

00:47:49.760 --> 00:48:07.810
<v Elmo>And at the end of our trip through Fort Marcy Park I said to him, you know, you've given me a very logical story, Patrick. I'm willing to believe what you're saying. You know, it's very logical. But I might just walk on. What do you think of that?
<v Elmo>在我们走完马西堡公园结束时，我对他说：“你知道吗，你给我讲了一个非常合逻辑的故事，帕特里克。我愿意相信你所说的。你知道，这很合逻辑。但我可能会继续前行。你怎么看？”

00:48:07.810 --> 00:48:11.880
<v Paul Tough>And what did he say?
<v Paul Tough>他说了什么？

00:48:11.880 --> 00:49:00.490
<v Philip Weiss>Well he just would say, and accept that-- in this Jimmy Stewart way. Gosh, Phil, and just accept that your government is telling you these lies about the most important death in high office since the Kennedy-- that kind of thing. I said, yeah. Yeah. Given my understanding of how the world works, I'm willing to accept that they have lied to me about this and that I got other things to do now. And I think he just kind of shook his head at that because that understanding is too central to his worldview to go move onto something else.
<v Philip Weiss>嗯，他只会用那种吉米·斯图尔特的口吻说：“天哪，菲尔，接受这一点吧——你的政府在关于自肯尼迪以来高层最重要的死亡事件上对你撒了谎——就是那种事。”我说：“是的。是的。鉴于我对世界运作方式的理解，我愿意接受他们在这件事上对我撒谎了，现在我还有别的事要做。”我想他只是摇了摇头，因为那种理解对于他的世界观来说太核心了，以至于无法去关注别的事情。

00:49:00.490 --> 00:49:46.870
<v Ira Glass>Philip Weiss is a novelist and columnist for the New York Observer . He spoke with This American Life producer Paul Tough.
<v Ira Glass>菲利普·韦斯是《纽约观察家》的小说家和专栏作家。他接受了《美国生活》制片人保罗·塔夫的采访。

00:49:46.870 --> 00:50:11.040
<v Ira Glass>Act Five, Quitting Quitting. Back in 1994, Evan Harris quit everything in her life. Job her, boyfriend, her city. And she started to think about quitting as an abstract idea in and of itself. She started to develop a theory about quitting. She wanted to name the parts of quitting. And she started to publish a zine about quiting with a friend of hers. She's also published a book about quitting called, The Quit.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，戒掉戒除。1994年，埃文·哈里斯戒掉了她生活中的一切。她的工作，她的男朋友，她所在的城市。她开始把“戒除”本身作为一个抽象理念来思考。她开始发展出一套关于戒除的理论。她想给戒除的各个部分命名。她开始和朋友一起出版一本关于戒除的杂志。她还出版了一本关于戒除的书，名为《戒》。

00:50:11.040 --> 00:50:28.640
<v Ira Glass>The more Evan Harris looked around, the more it seemed like everything in the world was made up of quits and quitting. History seemed like a series of violent, elaborate quits by nations and peoples. People's personal lives seemed driven by a series of endings. Quitting became the lens, the one thing through which Evan Harris saw the world.
<v Ira Glass>埃文·哈里斯看得越多，就越觉得世界上的一切都是由戒除和戒掉组成的。历史看起来像是国家和民族一系列剧烈的、精细的戒除。个人的生活似乎是由一系列结束所驱动的。戒除成为了棱镜，成为埃文·哈里斯看待世界的那“一件事”。

00:50:28.640 --> 00:51:06.220
<v Evan Harris>The point of quitting is to move in the world and to get bigger and bigger as a person. See, God damn. Sometimes I just start to sound like a nut basically about this. Just what am I talking about? You know, what am I talking about? You have to understand, this is so on my mind. You know, I mean I think about this all the time. I think about this all the time. I'm thinking about getting up and walking out of this room right now. You know, I really am.
<v Evan Harris>戒除的重点在于世界中移动，并作为一个人的规模变得越来越大。看，该死。有时我只是听起来像个关于此事的疯子。我到底在说什么？你知道，我到底在说什么？你必须明白，这太让我挂心了。你知道，我的意思是，我一直都在想这个。我一直都在想这个。我正想着从这把椅子上站起来走出这个房间。你知道，我真的在想。

00:51:06.220 --> 00:51:21.160
<v Ira Glass>That tape if from when I interviewed Evan Harris back in 1994. This is back when she made her big sets of quits. She was at the height of her quitting frenzy and her thinking about quitting. At the time she was trying to catalog still all the parts of quitting, the euphoria of the quit, the euphoria that you feel when you get a quit.
<v Ira Glass>那段磁带是我在1994年采访埃文·哈里斯时的内容。那是她做出那一系列大戒除的时候。她正处于戒除狂热和对戒除思考的顶峰。当时她还在试图给戒除的所有部分分类，戒除的快感，当你得到一次戒除时你感受到的快感。

00:51:21.160 --> 00:51:32.890
<v Ira Glass>Well now, Evan Harris has quit quitting. Three years after that. Or it quit her. She no longer is in the throes of one thing. I talked to her about it last week.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，现在，埃文·哈里斯已经戒掉了戒除。在那之后三年。或者说是它戒掉了她。她不再处于“一件事”的阵痛中。我上周和她聊了聊。

00:51:32.890 --> 00:52:23.350
<v Evan Harris>It was really horrible. It was very, very horrible. And it actually happened in a very, very dramatic way. I was taking this cross country trip and I stopped traveling with the person with whom I was traveling, which was a very big deal. And I was sitting on a bus, a Greyhound bus, traveling from Santa Fe, New Mexico, and I could no longer think about the trip in terms of quitting because a quit of sorts had occurred and the person with whom I was splitting was too important to funnel into the quitting stream.
<v Evan Harris>那真的很可怕。非常，非常可怕。而且它实际上以一种非常，非常戏剧性的方式发生了。我当时正进行长途旅行，我停止了与和我一起旅行的那个人同行，这是一个非常大的事情。我当时正坐在一辆巴士上，一辆灰狗巴士，从新墨西哥州的圣达菲出发，我再也无法用“戒除”来思考这次旅行了，因为某种戒除已经发生了，而与我分手的那个人太重要了，无法被挤进“戒除流”中。

00:52:23.350 --> 00:52:32.530
<v Ira Glass>Because the quitting streaming includes the idea of that when you quit you will feel good? That's almost like one of your definitions of quitting from back then.
<v Ira Glass>因为“戒除流”包括了当你戒除时你会感觉良好的想法？那几乎像是你当时的戒除定义之一。

00:52:32.530 --> 00:52:49.950
<v Evan Harris>There's that on one level, but then there's an even more serious level, which was that it was not possible to think of this as anything but what it was. That this was a relationship with a person who I love.
<v Evan Harris>那一层有一点，但还有一个更严肃的层面，那就是不可能把这看作除了它本身以外的任何事情。这是一段与我所爱之人的关系。

00:52:49.950 --> 00:52:52.210
<v Ira Glass>Right. And it wasn't just this theoretical quit.
<v Ira Glass>对。而不仅仅是这个理论上的戒除。

00:52:52.210 --> 00:52:56.930
<v Evan Harris>Right. There was nothing theoretical about it. I was on a Greyhound bus.
<v Evan Harris>对。没什么理论上的东西。我当时正坐在灰狗巴士上。

00:52:56.930 --> 00:53:00.370
<v Ira Glass>And so then the fever broke?
<v Ira Glass>所以在那之后高烧就退了？

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:05.120
<v Evan Harris>Then the fever broke.
<v Evan Harris>那时高烧退了。

00:53:05.120 --> 00:53:12.350
<v Ira Glass>When quitting was all you thought about, back when quitting was all you thought about, what was that like now that you look back on it?
<v Ira Glass>当戒除是你全部的想法时，回到你全部都想的是戒除的时候，现在回想起来那是什么感觉？

00:53:12.350 --> 00:53:31.610
<v Evan Harris>Oh, it was great. It was great. My mind was jumping all the time. And everything was fitting in. It was like a puzzle. It was like figuring out a puzzle. A big, jigsaw puzzle. And I would fit a piece in and then things would start to fit around it.
<v Evan Harris>哦，那太棒了。那太棒了。我的大脑一直在跳动。而且一切都吻合了。就像破解一个谜题。就像破解一个谜题。一个巨大的拼图。我会把一块拼图放进去，然后事情就开始围绕它吻合起来。

00:53:31.610 --> 00:53:39.700
<v Ira Glass>When I hear this, I just picture you just sitting down and just pouring these pages out, page after page, like in a dream. Like in a seizure.
<v Ira Glass>当我听到这个时，我脑海中浮现出你只是坐下来，一页又一页地写下这些，就像在梦里一样。就像在发作一样。

00:53:39.700 --> 00:53:46.960
<v Evan Harris>Yes, it was automatic writing, Ira. It was automatic writing. It was like someone turned on a faucet.
<v Evan Harris>是的，那是自动书写，艾拉。那是自动书写。就像有人打开了水龙头。

00:53:46.960 --> 00:53:49.430
<v Ira Glass>See, but what in your life today can compete with that?
<v Ira Glass>你看，但你今天生活中有什么能与那个竞争吗？

00:53:49.430 --> 00:53:52.090
<v Evan Harris>Nothing. Oh, what? Say again.
<v Evan Harris>没有。哦，什么？再说一遍。

00:53:52.090 --> 00:53:53.850
<v Ira Glass>No, no, you were saying nothing.
<v Ira Glass>不，不，你在说没有。

00:53:53.850 --> 00:53:56.780
<v Evan Harris>I know, but I wanted to think about it more before I said that.
<v Evan Harris>我知道，但我说那句话之前我想再多思考一下。

00:53:56.780 --> 00:54:02.100
<v Ira Glass>Well, you know, like do you miss having that faucet going full force all the time?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，你知道，就像你怀念那个一直全力运转的水龙头吗？

00:54:02.100 --> 00:54:26.140
<v Evan Harris>Yeah, I do. I really do. Because there was electricity that was very exciting and I think I was much more interesting then. I mean, I think I'm a little bit of a bore sometimes now.
<v Evan Harris>是的，我怀念。我真的很怀念。因为有那种非常令人兴奋的电流，而且我觉得那时我有趣多了。我的意思是，我觉得我现在有时候有点无聊。

00:54:26.140 --> 00:54:34.240
<v Ira Glass>And now you don't organize your thinking about the world that way. Now everything doesn't come down to one thing. Everything is kind of everything?
<v Ira Glass>而现在你不那样组织你看待世界的方式了。现在不是所有事都归结为一件事了。一切都只是……一切？

00:54:34.240 --> 00:54:36.770
<v Evan Harris>Yes, everything is everything.
<v Evan Harris>是的，一切都是一切。

00:54:36.770 --> 00:54:48.440
<v Ira Glass>Do you think that you lost something by now seeing everything in the world as everything and not just boiling everything down to one thing?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得你因为现在把世界看作一切，而不是把一切简化为一件事，而失去了什么吗？

00:54:48.440 --> 00:54:50.640
<v Evan Harris>Yes.
<v Evan Harris>是的。

00:54:50.640 --> 00:54:52.540
<v Ira Glass>What?
<v Ira Glass>什么？

00:54:52.540 --> 00:55:02.090
<v Evan Harris>I am no longer unified. I was unified and now I'm not unified anymore.
<v Evan Harris>我不再统一了。我曾经是统一的，现在不再统一了。

00:55:02.090 --> 00:55:07.960
<v Ira Glass>Unified, you mean everything added up to one idea?
<v Ira Glass>统一，你的意思是所有东西都加起来等于一个理念？

00:55:07.960 --> 00:55:26.550
<v Evan Harris>Right. I was able to navigate inside of me in terms of that one thing. So I was a unified person. And I actually had an identity.
<v Evan Harris>对。我能够在那一件事的条件下导航我的内心。所以我曾经是一个统一的人。而且我实际上有一个身份。

00:55:26.550 --> 00:55:35.180
<v Ira Glass>Can I ask you what else what else have you lost by giving up that phase of your life when you really believed?
<v Ira Glass>我可以问你，通过放弃你真正相信的那段人生阶段，你还失去了什么吗？

00:55:35.180 --> 00:55:50.970
<v Evan Harris>Looking at it that way makes it seem like I sacrificed something by moving away from it. But I didn't pick to move away from it. I mean, it abandoned me, Ira. It abandoned me. Really, it did.
<v Evan Harris>这样看，它看起来好像是我通过离开它而牺牲了什么。但我不是选择离开它的。我的意思是，它抛弃了我，艾拉。它抛弃了我。真的，它确实。

00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:53.550
<v Ira Glass>You mean it stopped seeming so interesting?
<v Ira Glass>你是说它不再看起来那么有趣了？

00:55:53.550 --> 00:56:09.270
<v Evan Harris>No, it stopped being available to me. It's not that I decided to stop thinking about it, it's that it decided to stop visiting me. It stopped calling me. It was terrible. It was horrible.
<v Evan Harris>不，它不再对我可用了。并不是我决定停止思考它，而是它决定停止造访我。它停止给我打电话了。那太可怕了。太可怕了。

00:56:09.270 --> 00:56:46.820
<v Evan Harris>There was a time actually, where I was very angry with the one thing. Because it had abandoned me and it was not available to me. It was like the quit and I had been friends, and then something happened and I didn't know what and I had done something terrible and the quit didn't come to my birthday party for no reason. And yeah, wouldn't call me and wouldn't write to me. And just disappeared, vanished, evaporated, abandoned me.
<v Evan Harris>实际上有一段时间，我非常生那件事的气。因为它抛弃了我，而且不再对我可用了。就像“戒除”和我曾经是朋友，然后发生了什么，我做了什么可怕的事情，而“戒除”没有理由地没来参加我的生日派对。而且是的，不会给我打电话，也不会给我写信。只是消失了，蒸发了，抛弃了我。

00:56:46.820 --> 00:56:59.710
<v Ira Glass>And so you used to see quitting as being the engine behind everything around us. Do you feel like that is the best explanatory framework with which to understand your life?
<v Ira Glass>所以你曾经把戒除看作我们周围一切事物的推动力。你觉得那是理解你生活的最佳解释框架吗？

00:56:59.710 --> 00:57:11.040
<v Evan Harris>Yes and no. Yes and no. The sad thing is that I don't really have a scheme. I attempted to embrace another framework.
<v Evan Harris>是，也不是。是，也不是。可悲的是，我真的没有一个方案。我试图拥抱另一个框架。

00:57:11.040 --> 00:57:11.650
<v Ira Glass>You did?
<v Ira Glass>你尝试了？

00:57:11.650 --> 00:57:12.250
<v Evan Harris>Yes.
<v Evan Harris>是的。

00:57:12.250 --> 00:57:12.960
<v Ira Glass>What was that?
<v Ira Glass>那是什么？

00:57:12.960 --> 00:57:14.150
<v Evan Harris>Picking.
<v Evan Harris>挑选。

00:57:14.150 --> 00:57:14.940
<v Ira Glass>Picking?
<v Ira Glass>挑选？

00:57:14.940 --> 00:57:17.520
<v Evan Harris>Picking.
<v Evan Harris>挑选。

00:57:17.520 --> 00:57:19.050
<v Ira Glass>Meaning what?
<v Ira Glass>意思是什么？

00:57:19.050 --> 00:57:36.930
<v Evan Harris>Picking anything. See, I'm a big picker. I'm a picker. And also, I like picking. For example, I once had a job as a strawberry picker, which I enjoyed very much. But then, that kind of picking. But also, the kind of picking, picking and choosing.
<v Evan Harris>挑选任何东西。看，我是一个大的挑选者。我是一个挑选者。而且，我喜欢挑选。例如，我曾经有一份工作是草莓挑选员，我非常享受。但那是那种挑选。还有，那种挑选，精挑细选。

00:57:36.930 --> 00:57:48.290
<v Ira Glass>Well, I mean as you say that, I mean it occurs to me that this whole scheme is just the mirror opposite of quitting, really. I mean, it's just somebody picking stuff instead of somebody quitting stuff, right?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我的意思是当你这么说时，我想到的是，整个方案只是戒除的镜面反向，真的。我的意思是，它只是某人挑选东西而不是某人戒掉东西，对吧？

00:57:48.290 --> 00:57:55.700
<v Evan Harris>Right, I guess so.
<v Evan Harris>对，我想是这样。

00:57:55.700 --> 00:57:57.220
<v Ira Glass>Evan Harris.
<v Ira Glass>埃文·哈里斯。

00:57:57.220 --> 00:58:06.040
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Nancy Updike and myself with Paul Tough, Alix Spiegel, Julie Snyder, and special guest producer Kitty Eisley. Contributing editors Sarah Vowell, Jack Hitt and Margy Rockland.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由南希·厄普代克和我制作，保罗·塔夫、阿利克斯·斯皮格尔、朱莉·斯奈德和特邀制作人凯蒂·艾斯利参与。特约编辑莎拉·沃威尔、杰克·希特和玛吉·洛克兰。

